{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JjYeyyVkGDuHNzoKpqJrk9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"adapters/codex/run -- see README.md (this directory) for the two\n3\tconfiguration keys, and ../README.md for the shared adapter contract.\n4\t\n5\tThe capability read (issue #41, recorded on the issue 2026-09-08 against\n6\tcodex-cli 0.153.4): `codex exec review --output-schema <FILE>` ignores\n7\tthe schema entirely -- confirmed live against a real planted-defect\n8\tdiff, its `-o` output is always free-text prose, never JSON, whatever\n9\tschema is named. This adapter therefore never invokes `codex exec\n10\treview`; both stages feed their own instruction to plain `codex exec -`\n11\ton stdin (confirmed live: this path does honor --output-schema, and\n12\t`-p` is codex's `--profile`, not a prompt flag, so stdin is the only\n13\tchannel and it is a positional `-`, not a flag).\n14\t\n15\tTwo channels, not one. `--json` prints events to stdout as JSONL\n16\t(thread.started, turn.started, item.*, and a terminal turn.completed or\n17\tturn.failed carrying usage) -- read for the model's own account of\n18\tusage and for the terminal event; codex's own exit code is 1 on\n19\tturn.failed, 0 on turn.completed, read directly, so this adapter reads\n20\tthe terminal event for the reason, not for the verdict. The structured\n21\tanswer comes from `--output-schema <FILE>` plus `--output-last-message\n22\t<FILE>`, written as one JSON object once the turn completes -- that is\n23\twhere findings and tests_named come from; parsing them out of the event\n24\tstream would be the wrong shape.\n25\t\n26\tNo model id appears anywhere codex reports -- not the event stream, not\n27\t`codex doctor`, not any config file (confirmed live 2026-09-08; there is\n28\tno ~/.codex/config.toml on this machine at all). [executor.codex].model\n29\tis therefore required, not optional: this adapter refuses before ever\n30\tinvoking codex if it is absent, and reports back exactly the id it was\n31\tgiven, never one derived from output.\n32\t\n33\tauth mirrors claude-code's auth = \"subscription\": the operator's own\n34\tmachine is logged in via a ChatGPT plan, and an OPENAI_API_KEY left in\n35\tthe environment would silently switch codex to API billing on a run\n36\tmeant for the plan. [executor.codex].auth names the mode this\n37\tinvocation is trusted to run under, checked against OPENAI_API_KEY\n38\talone (deterministic and testable without a live `codex doctor` call,\n39\twhich a canned-response or faked-tool_binary test situation cannot\n40\tanswer honestly anyway) -- this adapter refuses rather than silently\n41\tfalling back when they disagree, because a wrong credential here is a\n42\tbill, not just a wrong answer. cost_usd is therefore always null on\n43\tthis adapter (no dollar figure appears anywhere under plan auth, and\n44\tnone is invented for API auth either): a codex record carrying a\n45\tnon-null cost would be evidence the wrong credential was used, a\n46\tfinding about the credential, not a measurement.\n47\t\n48\tNot exercised live by the test suite (judgment is measured, not\n49\ttested); its argument construction is checked by ATTAVO_ADAPTER_DRY_RUN\n50\t(prints {\"argv\", \"instruction\", \"schema\"} instead of running -- argv\n51\talone would carry no instruction, since it goes in on stdin), and its\n52\tresponse handling is checked by ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE, which\n53\tfeeds a canned {\"events\": [...JSONL objects...], \"final\": {...the\n54\toutput-file's parsed content, or null...}, \"exit_code\": N} instead of\n55\tinvoking codex -- codex's own canned-response hook, since codex itself\n56\thas none (issue #41). A JSON array of such objects is a queue, one per\n57\tinvocation this adapter makes, its last element repeating once\n58\texhausted; a single object still repeats for every call (issue #88: the\n59\tnon-question retry below is a second invocation inside the same run).\n60\t\n61\tIssue #88 (class A: a report is not a question). Exactly claude-code's\n62\town fix, against the same build-side status/question channel: a\n63\t\"question\" whose text clears MIN_QUESTION_CHARS but carries no \"?\" is a\n64\treport, not a question, and this adapter re-invokes codex once more\n65\t(REINSTRUCTION appended to the same instruction) before ever reporting a\n66\thalt. A second report in a row still halts -- someone must look -- but\n67\tits report line carries \"class\": \"non-question\" and both raw results\n68\talready reached stdout above it.\n69\t\n70\tIssue #88 (class B: work that outlives the invocation). `codex exec`'s\n71\town surface -- `codex exec --help` (codex-cli 0.153.4), the JSONL event\n72\tstream (thread.*, turn.*, item.*), and the --output-schema/\n73\t--output-last-message response -- names no subagent or background-task\n74\tconcept at all: codex is a single agent driving sandboxed shell\n75\tcommands, with no tool of its own that could spawn work outliving this\n76\tinvocation the way claude-code's Agent/Workflow tools did (issue #88,\n77\tconfirmed live on claude-code, this adapter's own conformance.json\n78\tdeclares codex's guard unsupported for exactly this reason rather than\n79\tasserting a check against a concept that is not there).\n80\t\"\"\"\n81\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n82\t\n83\timport json\n84\timport os\n85\timport subprocess\n86\timport sys\n87\timport tempfile\n88\tfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\n89\tfrom pathlib import Path\n90\t\n91\t# the helper beside adapters/, imported without leaving a __pycache__ there\n92\tsys.dont_write_bytecode = True\n93\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent))\n94\tfrom executor_env import executor_env  # noqa: E402\n95\t\n96\tSTATUS_SCHEMA = {\n97\t    \"type\": \"object\",\n98\t    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n99\t    \"required\": [\"status\", \"question\"],\n100\t    \"properties\": {\n101\t        \"status\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"done\", \"question\"]},\n102\t        \"question\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n103\t    },\n104\t}\n105\t\n106\tVERIFY_SCHEMA = {\n107\t    \"type\": \"object\",\n108\t    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n109\t    \"required\": [\"findings\", \"tests_named\"],\n110\t    \"properties\": {\n111\t        \"findings\": {\n112\t            \"type\": \"array\",\n113\t            \"items\": {\n114\t                \"type\": \"object\",\n115\t                \"additionalProperties\": False,\n116\t                \"required\": [\"title\", \"specimen\", \"retires\", \"rejection_test\", \"severity\"],\n117\t                \"properties\": {\n118\t                    \"title\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n119\t                    \"specimen\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n120\t                    \"retires\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n121\t                    \"rejection_test\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n122\t                    \"severity\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n123\t                },\n124\t            },\n125\t        },\n126\t        \"tests_named\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1}},\n127\t    },\n128\t}\n129\t\n130\t# The counsel objective (issue #100): a stop is read inside the product\n131\t# first, before a person. Identical to claude-code's own COUNSEL_SCHEMA --\n132\t# the counsel structured answer is the shared adapter contract, not\n133\t# either tool's own shape.\n134\tCOUNSEL_SCHEMA = {\n135\t    \"type\": \"object\",\n136\t    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n137\t    \"required\": [\"class\", \"verdict\", \"citations\", \"proposal\"],\n138\t    \"properties\": {\n139\t        \"class\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n140\t        \"verdict\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"answer\", \"patch\", \"person\"]},\n141\t        \"citations\": {\n142\t            \"type\": \"array\",\n143\t            \"items\": {\n144\t                \"type\": \"object\",\n145\t                \"additionalProperties\": False,\n146\t                \"required\": [\"kind\", \"path\", \"symbol\", \"ruling_id\"],\n147\t                \"properties\": {\n148\t                    \"kind\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"instruction\", \"mechanism\", \"guard\"]},\n149\t                    \"path\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n150\t                    \"symbol\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n151\t                    \"ruling_id\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n152\t                },\n153\t            },\n154\t        },\n155\t        \"proposal\": {\n156\t            \"type\": \"object\",\n157\t            \"additionalProperties\": False,\n158\t            \"required\": [\"ruling_text\", \"patch\", \"question\", \"issue\"],\n159\t            \"properties\": {\n160\t                \"ruling_text\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n161\t                \"patch\": {\n162\t                    \"type\": [\"object\", \"null\"],\n163\t                    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n164\t                    \"required\": [\"file\", \"symbol\", \"rejection_test\", \"diff\"],\n165\t                    \"properties\": {\n166\t                        \"file\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n167\t                        \"symbol\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n168\t                        \"rejection_test\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n169\t                        \"diff\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n170\t                    },\n171\t                },\n172\t                \"question\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n173\t                # issue #175: the fourth proposal shape, for a \"patch\" or\n174\t                # \"person\" verdict whose remedy is better filed as an item\n175\t                # than shipped as a diff or asked as a bare question.\n176\t                \"issue\": {\n177\t                    \"type\": [\"object\", \"null\"],\n178\t                    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n179\t                    \"required\": [\"title\", \"body\"],\n180\t                    \"properties\": {\n181\t                        \"title\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n182\t                        \"body\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n183\t                    },\n184\t                },\n185\t            },\n186\t        },\n187\t    },\n188\t}\n189\t\n190\t\n191\tdef read_text(path_env: str) -> str:\n192\t    path = os.environ.get(path_env)\n193\t    if not path:\n194\t        return \"\"\n195\t    p = Path(path)\n196\t    return p.read_text() if p.is_file() else \"\"\n197\t\n198\t\n199\tdef describe_file(path: str, text: str, diff_like: bool) -> str:\n200\t    \"\"\"Path, byte count, line count, and (for a diff) files touched --\n201\t    named in the instruction instead of inlining the file's own text, so\n202\t    the instruction stays a small fixed size whatever the file's size\n203\t    (issue #86: a 27,367-line deletion diff inlined whole exceeded\n204\t    codex's own input ceiling). Files touched counts unified-diff file\n205\t    headers (`diff --git a/... b/...`), the shape `git diff` produces.\"\"\"\n206\t    size = Path(path).stat().st_size\n207\t    lines = text.count(\"\\n\") + (1 if text and not text.endswith(\"\\n\") else 0)\n208\t    if not diff_like:\n209\t        return f\"`{path}` ({size} bytes, {lines} lines)\"\n210\t    files = sum(1 for l in text.splitlines() if l.startswith(\"diff --git \"))\n211\t    return f\"`{path}` ({size} bytes, {lines} lines, {files} files touched)\"\n212\t\n213\t\n214\tdef spec_sections() -> list[str]:\n215\t    \"\"\"The spec's Problem, Claims, and Criteria sections verbatim -- the\n216\t    same contract adapters/claude-code/run reads, since what a verifier\n217\t    must be told is a product fact, not a tool-specific one.\"\"\"\n218\t    spec_dir = Path(os.environ[\"ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR\"])\n219\t    lines = (spec_dir / \"spec.md\").read_text().splitlines()\n220\t    idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith(\"## \")]\n221\t    headings = {lines[i].strip(): i for i in idx}\n222\t    bounds = sorted(idx) + [len(lines)]\n223\t\n224\t    def section(heading: str) -> str:\n225\t        start = headings[heading]\n226\t        end = bounds[bounds.index(start) + 1] if start in bounds else len(lines)\n227\t        return \"\\n\".join(lines[start:end]).strip()\n228\t\n229\t    return [section(\"## Problem\"), section(\"## Claims\"), section(\"## Criteria\")]\n230\t\n231\t\n232\tdef stub_note() -> str | None:\n233\t    \"\"\"None when ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR carries no stub/; else the sentence\n234\t    naming what a stub is (issue #90, closed by #93). The stub is\n235\t    evidence -- a criterion it satisfies trivially is weak -- but twice\n236\t    the judge reported\n237\t    a built tree as failing to match its stub as if the stub were the\n238\t    intended change (tenant zero #664, #644): one sentence of vocabulary\n239\t    was missing, not a smarter judge or a dropped stub.\"\"\"\n240\t    stub_dir = Path(os.environ[\"ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR\"]) / \"stub\"\n241\t    if not stub_dir.is_dir():\n242\t        return None\n243\t    return (\n244\t        f\"## The stub at `{stub_dir}`\\n\\n\"\n245\t        \"The stub is the fixture the acceptance criteria were shown to \"\n246\t        \"pass against; it is not the intended change. A difference \"\n247\t        \"between the built tree and the stub is not a finding: a \"\n248\t        \"criterion the stub satisfies trivially is.\")\n249\t\n250\t\n251\tdef build_instruction() -> str:\n252\t    parts = list(spec_sections())\n253\t\n254\t    rulings = read_text(\"ATTAVO_RULINGS\")\n255\t    if rulings.strip():\n256\t        parts.append(\"## Rulings\\n\\n\" + rulings.strip())\n257\t\n258\t    feedback_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_FEEDBACK\")\n259\t    feedback = read_text(\"ATTAVO_FEEDBACK\")\n260\t    if feedback.strip():\n261\t        parts.append(\n262\t            \"## Feedback from the previous attempt\\n\\n\"\n263\t            f\"The feedback is at {describe_file(feedback_path, feedback, diff_like=False)}. \"\n264\t            \"It is not inlined here: read it yourself with your own tools \"\n265\t            \"before continuing.\")\n266\t\n267\t    parts.append(\n268\t        \"commit your work on the current branch; if you cannot proceed \"\n269\t        \"without a decision that changes the criteria, do not guess: \"\n270\t        \"answer with status question\")\n271\t\n272\t    test_command = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_TEST_COMMAND\")\n273\t    if test_command:\n274\t        bound = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_TEST_TIMEOUT_S\")\n275\t        parts.append(\n276\t            f\"to run tests while you work, run `{test_command}`: it runs the tests \"\n277\t            \"selected for the files you have changed\"\n278\t            + (f\" and stops at {bound} s\" if bound else \"\")\n279\t            + \". Do not run the whole test suite or any whole-tree check yourself: \"\n280\t            \"after you commit, the gates are run for you and any failure is \"\n281\t            \"returned to you on the next iteration\")\n282\t\n283\t    return \"\\n\\n\".join(parts)\n284\t\n285\t\n286\tdef verify_instruction() -> str:\n287\t    parts = list(spec_sections())\n288\t\n289\t    note = stub_note()\n290\t    if note:\n291\t        parts.append(note)\n292\t\n293\t    diff_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_DIFF\")\n294\t    diff = read_text(\"ATTAVO_DIFF\")\n295\t    if diff.strip():\n296\t        parts.append(\n297\t            \"## Diff since the approved spec\\n\\n\"\n298\t            f\"The diff is at {describe_file(diff_path, diff, diff_like=True)}. \"\n299\t            \"It is not inlined here: read it yourself with your own tools \"\n300\t            \"before judging it.\")\n301\t    else:\n302\t        parts.append(\"## Diff since the approved spec\\n\\n(the diff is empty)\")\n303\t\n304\t    build_record_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD\")\n305\t    build_record = read_text(\"ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD\")\n306\t    if build_record.strip():\n307\t        parts.append(\n308\t            \"## The build record\\n\\n\"\n309\t            f\"The build record is at {describe_file(build_record_path, build_record, diff_like=False)}. \"\n310\t            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n311\t\n312\t    parts.append(\n313\t        \"you are verifying a completed build, not building: make no edits, \"\n314\t        \"write no files, commit nothing. Read the diff named above against \"\n315\t        \"the criteria and name what the criteria do not assert but the diff \"\n316\t        \"should still be judged on, and name the tests that would assert \"\n317\t        \"it if they existed. A spec whose approval commit does not \"\n318\t        \"describe the working tree the build produced is itself a \"\n319\t        \"finding: the diff is judged against what was approved, and a \"\n320\t        \"divergence between them is reportable rather than assumed \"\n321\t        \"away. Report each as a finding: title, specimen (the file or \"\n322\t        \"commit the finding is about), retires (what having this \"\n323\t        \"finding on record replaces or closes -- every finding must \"\n324\t        \"carry one, or it is refused unfiled), rejection_test (the test \"\n325\t        \"that would catch a regression of it), and severity. Severity \"\n326\t        \"is derived rather than judged: the version, counter, \"\n327\t        \"condition, or state change that flips a finding from harmless \"\n328\t        \"to blocking; an adjective is not a severity. An empty \"\n329\t        \"findings list means you looked and found nothing to report, not \"\n330\t        \"that you did not look.\")\n331\t    return \"\\n\\n\".join(parts)\n332\t\n333\t\n334\tdef counsel_instruction() -> str:\n335\t    \"\"\"Issue #100 (extended by issue #175): a stop is read inside the\n336\t    product first, before a person. Identical to claude-code's own\n337\t    counsel_instruction -- the record, its log, the diff, the forge's\n338\t    checks, the item's graph, the tenant manifest, and this product's own\n339\t    root are all handed by path, named rather than inlined, the same\n340\t    discipline verify_instruction already applies to the diff (issue\n341\t    #86).\"\"\"\n342\t    parts = list(spec_sections())\n343\t\n344\t    rulings = read_text(\"ATTAVO_RULINGS\")\n345\t    if rulings.strip():\n346\t        parts.append(\"## Rulings\\n\\n\" + rulings.strip())\n347\t\n348\t    note = stub_note()\n349\t    if note:\n350\t        parts.append(note)\n351\t\n352\t    record_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_RECORD\")\n353\t    record_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_RECORD\")\n354\t    if record_text.strip():\n355\t        parts.append(\n356\t            \"## The stage record\\n\\n\"\n357\t            f\"The record is at {describe_file(record_path, record_text, diff_like=False)}. \"\n358\t            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n359\t\n360\t    log_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_LOG\")\n361\t    log_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_LOG\")\n362\t    if log_text.strip():\n363\t        parts.append(\n364\t            \"## The record's log\\n\\n\"\n365\t            f\"The log is at {describe_file(log_path, log_text, diff_like=False)}. \"\n366\t            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n367\t\n368\t    diff_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_DIFF\")\n369\t    diff_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_DIFF\")\n370\t    if diff_text.strip():\n371\t        parts.append(\n372\t            \"## The item's diff since approval\\n\\n\"\n373\t            f\"The diff is at {describe_file(diff_path, diff_text, diff_like=True)}. \"\n374\t            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n375\t\n376\t    # issue #175: the forge in the evidence set. ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS is\n377\t    # always written, a JSON {read, reason, checks} envelope -- read false\n378\t    # names the missing manifest key rather than leaving the section out,\n379\t    # since an unread forge must never be mistaken for a green one.\n380\t    checks_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS\")\n381\t    checks_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS\")\n382\t    if checks_text.strip():\n383\t        parts.append(\n384\t            \"## The forge's checks on this stop's sha\\n\\n\"\n385\t            f\"The checks are at {describe_file(checks_path, checks_text, diff_like=False)}: \"\n386\t            \"a JSON object -- read: false means the forge was not read at \"\n387\t            \"all (reason names the missing manifest key; treat this as \"\n388\t            \"unknown, never as green) -- read: true carries one entry per \"\n389\t            \"check, each naming, for a check that did not succeed, the \"\n390\t            \"path of its own failed-step log. Read the failing check's \"\n391\t            \"log before answering about the forge; a remedy that depends \"\n392\t            \"on the forge's state cites the check and its log line.\")\n393\t\n394\t    # issue #175: the graph in the evidence set -- the default branch's\n395\t    # own log since this item's branch point, this branch's own commits,\n396\t    # the markers and records present at the stop's sha, and the check\n397\t    # runs on the last three shas, so \"the checks are on the record\n398\t    # commit, not the code commit\" is readable without a terminal.\n399\t    graph_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_GRAPH\")\n400\t    graph_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_GRAPH\")\n401\t    if graph_text.strip():\n402\t        parts.append(\n403\t            \"## The item's graph\\n\\n\"\n404\t            f\"The graph is at {describe_file(graph_path, graph_text, diff_like=False)}. \"\n405\t            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n406\t\n407\t    manifest_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_MANIFEST\", \"\")\n408\t    parts.append(f\"## The tenant manifest\\n\\nThe manifest is at `{manifest_path}`. \"\n409\t                 \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n410\t\n411\t    product_root = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_PRODUCT_ROOT\", \"\")\n412\t    parts.append(\n413\t        \"## The product's own root\\n\\n\"\n414\t        f\"The product that ran this stage is at `{product_root}` \"\n415\t        \"(`bin/attavo`, `adapters/`, `schema/`, `templates/`). A stop \"\n416\t        \"whose cause is the product's own mechanism, not the tenant's, \"\n417\t        \"is read here.\")\n418\t\n419\t    # issue #175: attavo counsel N --ask hands the operator's own text as\n420\t    # the question to answer, over the same evidence set, rather than\n421\t    # diagnosing a stop -- the record above is context, not necessarily a\n422\t    # failure.\n423\t    question = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_QUESTION\", \"\")\n424\t    if question.strip():\n425\t        parts.append(\n426\t            \"## The operator's question\\n\\n\" + question.strip() +\n427\t            \"\\n\\nAnswer this question using the evidence above; the \"\n428\t            \"record named above is context, not necessarily a failure.\")\n429\t\n430\t    parts.append(\n431\t        \"you are counseling a stop, read-only: make no edits, write no \"\n432\t        \"files, commit nothing. Read the record, its log, the spec, the \"\n433\t        \"rulings, the diff, the forge's checks, the item's graph, the \"\n434\t        \"tenant manifest, and the product's own root named above with \"\n435\t        \"your own tools, then answer with exactly one class (the stop's \"\n436\t        \"class, or \\\"unclassed\\\"), one verdict -- \\\"answer\\\" (the stop is \"\n437\t        \"answerable inside the rules: a ruling text, in \"\n438\t        \"proposal.ruling_text, with three citations -- instruction, \"\n439\t        \"mechanism, guard, each a path and symbol or a ruling id), \"\n440\t        \"\\\"patch\\\" (the stop is a product defect: the mechanism's file \"\n441\t        \"and symbol, a rejection test, and a unified diff, in \"\n442\t        \"proposal.patch -- or, when the remedy is better filed as an \"\n443\t        \"item than shipped as a diff, a title and a body in this \"\n444\t        \"repository's own issue format -- a Severity/Provenance line, \"\n445\t        \"then Problem, Retires, Change, and Acceptance sections -- in \"\n446\t        \"proposal.issue), or \\\"person\\\" (the answer would touch the \"\n447\t        \"definition of done and no rule covers it: the question restated \"\n448\t        \"in one line, in proposal.question -- or, when the remedy is \"\n449\t        \"better filed as an item than asked as a bare question, a title \"\n450\t        \"and a body in the same issue format, in proposal.issue) -- and \"\n451\t        \"citations for whichever verdict you gave. You propose; you do \"\n452\t        \"not act.\")\n453\t    return \"\\n\\n\".join(parts)\n454\t\n455\t\n456\tdef load_config() -> dict:\n457\t    raw = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG\")\n458\t    return json.loads(raw) if raw else {}\n459\t\n460\t\n461\t# codex's own ceiling (issue #93): \"Input exceeds the maximum length of\n462\t# 1048576 characters\" -- confirmed live 2026-09-08 against codex-cli\n463\t# 0.153.4, the same specimen issue #86 fixed by handing the diff over by\n464\t# path (tenant zero #659, verify-01: input_too_large, max_chars 1048576,\n465\t# actual_chars 1297762). Declared here and in conformance.json's own\n466\t# input_limit so a test can assert the two agree.\n467\tINPUT_LIMIT_CHARS = 1_048_576\n468\t\n469\t\n470\tdef input_limit_chars() -> int:\n471\t    \"\"\"The declared ceiling, chars. Overridable only for tests via\n472\t    ATTAVO_ADAPTER_INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS -- never a tenant knob: the ceiling\n473\t    is a fact about codex, not something [executor.codex] can set (a\n474\t    tenant typing a number it does not know is the pattern rule 42\n475\t    refuses).\"\"\"\n476\t    override = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS\")\n477\t    return int(override) if override else INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS\n478\t\n479\t\n480\tdef ceiling_refusal(instruction: str) -> str | None:\n481\t    \"\"\"None if instruction stays at or under codex's declared ceiling;\n482\t    else the refusal naming both numbers, checked before codex is ever\n483\t    invoked -- so an oversized prompt never reaches the tool that would\n484\t    refuse it anyway, three seconds and a wasted call later.\"\"\"\n485\t    limit = input_limit_chars()\n486\t    size = len(instruction)\n487\t    if size <= limit:\n488\t        return None\n489\t    return (f\"ceiling exceeded -- instruction is {size} chars, over \"\n490\t           f\"codex's declared limit of {limit} chars\")\n491\t\n492\t\n493\tdef build_argv(schema_path: Path, output_path: Path, config: dict, stage: str) -> list[str]:\n494\t    # verify and counsel (issue #100) are mechanically read-only, not\n495\t    # just instructed to be: the sandbox enforces \"make no edits, write\n496\t    # no files, commit nothing\" rather than trusting the instruction\n497\t    # alone. Any stage other than \"build\" gets the read-only sandbox.\n498\t    sandbox = \"workspace-write\" if stage == \"build\" else \"read-only\"\n499\t    return [\"codex\", \"exec\", \"-\",\n500\t           \"--model\", config[\"model\"],\n501\t           \"--sandbox\", sandbox,\n502\t           \"--output-schema\", str(schema_path),\n503\t           \"--output-last-message\", str(output_path),\n504\t           \"--json\",\n505\t           \"--ignore-user-config\", \"--ignore-rules\"]\n506\t\n507\t\n508\tdef check_auth(config: dict) -> str | None:\n509\t    \"\"\"None if the declared auth mode and OPENAI_API_KEY's presence\n510\t    agree; else the mismatch, refused before codex is ever invoked.\"\"\"\n511\t    auth = config.get(\"auth\", \"subscription\")\n512\t    has_key = bool(os.environ.get(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"))\n513\t    if auth == \"subscription\" and has_key:\n514\t        return (\"auth is \\\"subscription\\\" but OPENAI_API_KEY is set in the \"\n515\t                \"environment -- codex would silently bill the API account \"\n516\t                \"instead of running under the plan\")\n517\t    if auth == \"api\" and not has_key:\n518\t        return \"auth is \\\"api\\\" but OPENAI_API_KEY is not set in the environment\"\n519\t    return None\n520\t\n521\t\n522\tdef parse_events(stdout: str) -> list[dict]:\n523\t    \"\"\"One JSON object per JSONL line; a tracing line (e.g. \"ERROR\n524\t    codex_api::...\") is not JSON and is skipped, not an error -- codex\n525\t    writes both to the same stream under --json.\"\"\"\n526\t    events = []\n527\t    for line in stdout.splitlines():\n528\t        line = line.strip()\n529\t        if not line:\n530\t            continue\n531\t        try:\n532\t            events.append(json.loads(line))\n533\t        except ValueError:\n534\t            continue\n535\t    return events\n536\t\n537\t\n538\tdef terminal_event(events: list[dict]) -> dict | None:\n539\t    for e in events:\n540\t        if e.get(\"type\") in (\"turn.completed\", \"turn.failed\"):\n541\t            return e\n542\t    return None\n543\t\n544\t\n545\tEVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS = 400\n546\t\n547\t\n548\tdef append_event(events_path: str, kind: str, text: str) -> None:\n549\t    \"\"\"One {at, kind, text} JSON line appended to events_path and\n550\t    flushed at once -- the same shape and the same open-write-close\n551\t    discipline bin/attavo's own append_event and claude-code's own copy\n552\t    use, kept independently here since an adapter is a separate process\n553\t    with no import path into the product or into another adapter.\"\"\"\n554\t    line = {\"at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\"),\n555\t           \"kind\": kind, \"text\": \" \".join(text.split())[:EVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS]}\n556\t    with open(events_path, \"a\") as fh:\n557\t        fh.write(json.dumps(line) + \"\\n\")\n558\t\n559\t\n560\tdef first_line(text: str) -> str:\n561\t    stripped = text.strip()\n562\t    return stripped.splitlines()[0] if stripped else \"\"\n563\t\n564\t\n565\tdef item_events(obj: dict) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:\n566\t    \"\"\"[(kind, text), ...] for one parsed codex --json stream line --\n567\t    zero, one, or two (a command_execution item carries both the\n568\t    command and, once it has run, its own output, so it maps to a\n569\t    tool_call and a tool_result together). An agent_message item's own\n570\t    text maps to \"message\" -- confirmed live (issue #41, recorded\n571\t    2026-09-08 against codex-cli 0.153.4): conformance.json's own\n572\t    \"done\" situation, `{\"type\": \"item.completed\", \"item\": {\"type\":\n573\t    \"agent_message\", \"text\": \"ok\"}}`, is this exact shape. A\n574\t    command_execution item's \"command\" maps to \"tool_call\" (the\n575\t    command's own first line) and its \"aggregated_output\", once\n576\t    present, to \"tool_result\" (the output's own first line) -- both\n577\t    field names are confirmed, by tenant zero's own verify logs on\n578\t    attavo/code-factory since #649: every command_execution item there\n579\t    carries exactly the keys aggregated_output, command, exit_code, id,\n580\t    status, type, the same shape tests/test_adapter_codex.py's recorded\n581\t    fixture exercises this mapping through. Every other item type\n582\t    (reasoning, file_change, mcp_tool_call, and the rest), and every\n583\t    non-item.completed line (thread.*, turn.*, \"result\" itself), maps\n584\t    to nothing here.\"\"\"\n585\t    if obj.get(\"type\") != \"item.completed\":\n586\t        return []\n587\t    item = obj.get(\"item\") or {}\n588\t    kind = item.get(\"type\")\n589\t    events: list[tuple[str, str]] = []\n590\t    if kind == \"agent_message\" and item.get(\"text\"):\n591\t        events.append((\"message\", first_line(item[\"text\"])))\n592\t    elif kind == \"command_execution\":\n593\t        command = item.get(\"command\")\n594\t        if command:\n595\t            events.append((\"tool_call\", first_line(str(command))))\n596\t        output = item.get(\"aggregated_output\")\n597\t        if output:\n598\t            events.append((\"tool_result\", first_line(str(output))))\n599\t    return events\n600\t\n601\t\n602\tdef write_events(events_path: str, parsed_events: list[dict]) -> None:\n603\t    for obj in parsed_events:\n604\t        for kind, text in item_events(obj):\n605\t            append_event(events_path, kind, text)\n606\t\n607\t\n608\tdef load_canned_responses() -> list[dict] | None:\n609\t    \"\"\"None when ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE is unset. Otherwise the\n610\t    queue of canned {\"events\", \"final\", \"exit_code\"} objects this run\n611\t    answers from: a single object repeats for every invocation this\n612\t    adapter makes (unchanged since v0.3.0 -- most tests make exactly\n613\t    one), a JSON array is consumed in order, one response per\n614\t    invocation, its last element repeating once exhausted (issue #88:\n615\t    the non-question retry is a second invocation inside the same\n616\t    adapter run, and a canned test needs to hand it a different answer\n617\t    than the first).\"\"\"\n618\t    response_file = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE\")\n619\t    if not response_file:\n620\t        return None\n621\t    raw = json.loads(Path(response_file).read_text())\n622\t    return raw if isinstance(raw, list) else [raw]\n623\t\n624\t\n625\tdef invoke(instruction: str, schema: dict, config: dict, stage: str,\n626\t          responses: list[dict] | None, call_index: int\n627\t          ) -> tuple[str, list[dict], dict | None, int]:\n628\t    \"\"\"(raw transcript text, parsed events, the output file's parsed\n629\t    content or None, exit code) -- from responses[call_index] (clamped\n630\t    to the last element) when responses is not None, else a real\n631\t    `codex exec -` invocation.\"\"\"\n632\t    if responses is not None:\n633\t        canned = responses[min(call_index, len(responses) - 1)]\n634\t        events = canned.get(\"events\") or []\n635\t        final = canned.get(\"final\")\n636\t        exit_code = canned.get(\"exit_code\", 0)\n637\t        raw = \"\\n\".join(json.dumps(e) for e in events)\n638\t        return raw, events, final, exit_code\n639\t\n640\t    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:\n641\t        schema_path = Path(tmp) / \"schema.json\"\n642\t        schema_path.write_text(json.dumps(schema))\n643\t        output_path = Path(tmp) / \"output.json\"\n644\t\n645\t        argv = build_argv(schema_path, output_path, config, stage)\n646\t        run = subprocess.run(argv, input=instruction, capture_output=True,\n647\t                             text=True, check=False, env=executor_env())\n648\t        raw = run.stdout\n649\t        if run.returncode != 0 and run.stderr.strip():\n650\t            raw = (raw + \"\\n\" if raw else \"\") + run.stderr.rstrip(\"\\n\")\n651\t\n652\t        final = None\n653\t        if output_path.is_file():\n654\t            try:\n655\t                final = json.loads(output_path.read_text())\n656\t            except ValueError:\n657\t                final = None\n658\t        return raw, parse_events(run.stdout), final, run.returncode\n659\t\n660\t\n661\tdef adapter_version() -> str:\n662\t    try:\n663\t        out = subprocess.run([\"codex\", \"--version\"], capture_output=True,\n664\t                             text=True, check=False)\n665\t    except OSError:\n666\t        return \"\"\n667\t    return out.stdout.strip()\n668\t\n669\t\n670\tMIN_QUESTION_CHARS = 40\n671\t\n672\tREINSTRUCTION = (\n673\t    \"your last answer was a report, not a question; either continue to \"\n674\t    \"done, or ask one question ending in a question mark that names the \"\n675\t    \"spec line or guard you cannot resolve\")\n676\t\n677\t\n678\tdef question_class(status: str, question: str | None) -> str:\n679\t    \"\"\"\"done\" (no halt at all), \"fail\" (a question too short or absent to\n680\t    be worth a person's judgment -- MIN_QUESTION_CHARS, unchanged since\n681\t    v0.15.0), \"report\" (long enough but carries no \"?\" -- issue #88's\n682\t    class A, a status update in the question channel), or \"question\" (a\n683\t    real question). Identical to claude-code's own -- the build-side\n684\t    status/question channel is the shared adapter contract, not either\n685\t    tool's own shape.\"\"\"\n686\t    if status != \"question\":\n687\t        return \"done\"\n688\t    if not question or len(question) < MIN_QUESTION_CHARS:\n689\t        # An absent or too-short question is a report with nothing in\n690\t        # it: a status update in the question channel (issue #150, five\n691\t        # specimens on 2026-09-17), never an exit with no report line.\n692\t        return \"report\"\n693\t    if \"?\" not in question:\n694\t        return \"report\"\n695\t    return \"question\"\n696\t\n697\t\n698\tdef usage_fields(events: list[dict]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n699\t    \"\"\"(tokens.input, tokens.output) for one call's own event stream --\n700\t    split out from report_fields so a retry's first, discarded call\n701\t    still counts (issue #88: a non-question costs two executor turns,\n702\t    and a report line built from the second call alone undercounts by\n703\t    exactly the first one).\"\"\"\n704\t    term = terminal_event(events)\n705\t    usage = (term or {}).get(\"usage\") or {}\n706\t    return usage.get(\"input_tokens\", 0) or 0, usage.get(\"output_tokens\", 0) or 0\n707\t\n708\t\n709\tdef report_fields(model: str, events: list[dict]) -> dict:\n710\t    \"\"\"The fields common to every final report line, derived from one\n711\t    call's own event stream -- model is always the configured id (issue\n712\t    #41: codex reports none of its own), cost_usd always null (issue\n713\t    #41's auth addendum).\"\"\"\n714\t    tokens_input, tokens_output = usage_fields(events)\n715\t    return {\n716\t        \"model\": model,\n717\t        \"model_version\": None,\n718\t        \"adapter_version\": adapter_version(),\n719\t        \"tokens\": {\"input\": tokens_input, \"output\": tokens_output},\n720\t        \"cost_usd\": None,\n721\t    }\n722\t\n723\t\n724\tdef main() -> int:\n725\t    config = load_config()\n726\t    stage = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_STAGE\", \"build\")\n727\t    verifying = stage == \"verify\"\n728\t    counseling = stage == \"counsel\"\n729\t    if verifying:\n730\t        instruction, schema = verify_instruction(), VERIFY_SCHEMA\n731\t    elif counseling:\n732\t        instruction, schema = counsel_instruction(), COUNSEL_SCHEMA\n733\t    else:\n734\t        instruction, schema = build_instruction(), STATUS_SCHEMA\n735\t\n736\t    model = config.get(\"model\")\n737\t    if not model:\n738\t        sys.stderr.write(\n739\t            \"adapters/codex/run: refused: [executor.codex].model is \"\n740\t            \"required -- no model id appears anywhere codex itself \"\n741\t            \"reports (the event stream, codex doctor, or any config file)\\n\")\n742\t        return 1\n743\t\n744\t    ceiling = ceiling_refusal(instruction)\n745\t    if ceiling:\n746\t        sys.stderr.write(f\"adapters/codex/run: refused: {ceiling}\\n\")\n747\t        return 1\n748\t\n749\t    if os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_DRY_RUN\"):\n750\t        argv = build_argv(Path(\"<schema.json>\"), Path(\"<output.json>\"), config, stage)\n751\t        print(json.dumps({\"argv\": argv, \"instruction\": instruction, \"schema\": schema}))\n752\t        return 0\n753\t\n754\t    if not os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE\"):\n755\t        auth_error = check_auth(config)\n756\t        if auth_error:\n757\t            sys.stderr.write(f\"adapters/codex/run: refused: {auth_error}\\n\")\n758\t            return 1\n759\t\n760\t    responses = load_canned_responses()\n761\t    events_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_EVENTS\")\n762\t\n763\t    raw, events, final, exit_code = invoke(instruction, schema, config, stage, responses, 0)\n764\t    # keep the evidence: both channels, not just the fields below, are\n765\t    # the transcript build-NN.log saves -- a halt otherwise cannot be\n766\t    # explained after the fact. final is printed here too (issue #88):\n767\t    # a question the executor answers on the first call otherwise never\n768\t    # reaches the transcript at all, since only the terminal report line\n769\t    # -- printed once, at the very end -- carried it before.\n770\t    print(raw.rstrip(\"\\n\") if raw.strip() else \"(codex produced no output)\")\n771\t    if final is not None:\n772\t        print(json.dumps(final))\n773\t    if events_path:\n774\t        write_events(events_path, events)\n775\t\n776\t    if exit_code != 0:\n777\t        return exit_code\n778\t\n779\t    # An absent or unparseable output file is a failure whether this is\n780\t    # a build or a verify -- the same class as claude-code's absent\n781\t    # structured_output.\n782\t    if final is None:\n783\t        return 1\n784\t\n785\t    if verifying:\n786\t        common = report_fields(model, events)\n787\t        line = {**common,\n788\t               \"findings\": final.get(\"findings\"),\n789\t               \"tests_named\": final.get(\"tests_named\")}\n790\t        print(json.dumps(line))\n791\t        return 0\n792\t\n793\t    if counseling:\n794\t        common = report_fields(model, events)\n795\t        line = {**common,\n796\t               \"class\": final.get(\"class\"),\n797\t               \"verdict\": final.get(\"verdict\"),\n798\t               \"citations\": final.get(\"citations\"),\n799\t               \"proposal\": final.get(\"proposal\")}\n800\t        print(json.dumps(line))\n801\t        return 0\n802\t\n803\t    status = final.get(\"status\", \"done\")\n804\t    question = final.get(\"question\")\n805\t    cls = question_class(status, question)\n806\t\n807\t    if cls == \"fail\":\n808\t        return 1\n809\t\n810\t    if cls == \"report\":\n811\t        # Issue #88, class A: a report in the question channel is\n812\t        # re-instructed once, in this same invocation, before it ever\n813\t        # reaches a person.\n814\t        reinstruction = instruction + \"\\n\\n\" + REINSTRUCTION\n815\t        raw2, events2, final2, exit_code2 = invoke(\n816\t            reinstruction, schema, config, stage, responses, 1)\n817\t        print(raw2.rstrip(\"\\n\") if raw2.strip() else \"(codex produced no output)\")\n818\t        if final2 is not None:\n819\t            print(json.dumps(final2))\n820\t        if events_path:\n821\t            write_events(events_path, events2)\n822\t\n823\t        if exit_code2 != 0:\n824\t            return exit_code2\n825\t        if final2 is None:\n826\t            return 1\n827\t\n828\t        status2 = final2.get(\"status\", \"done\")\n829\t        question2 = final2.get(\"question\")\n830\t        cls2 = question_class(status2, question2)\n831\t        if cls2 == \"fail\":\n832\t            return 1\n833\t\n834\t        common2 = report_fields(model, events2)\n835\t        # A non-question costs two executor turns, not one: sum both\n836\t        # calls' tokens into the one report line this invocation gets,\n837\t        # whatever the retry's own outcome. cost_usd stays null (issue\n838\t        # #41): null plus null is still null.\n839\t        tokens1_input, tokens1_output = usage_fields(events)\n840\t        common2[\"tokens\"][\"input\"] += tokens1_input\n841\t        common2[\"tokens\"][\"output\"] += tokens1_output\n842\t\n843\t        if cls2 == \"report\":\n844\t            # Two reports in a row: still a halt (someone must look),\n845\t            # but the transcript above already carries both raw\n846\t            # results, and this report line names the class so a\n847\t            # reader -- or a future record field, not this one -- can\n848\t            # tell it apart from a genuine question.\n849\t            print(\"adapters/codex/run: halted: class non-question -- the \"\n850\t                  \"executor reported instead of asking, twice in a row\")\n851\t            line = {**common2, \"status\": \"question\", \"question\": question2,\n852\t                   \"class\": \"non-question\"}\n853\t            print(json.dumps(line))\n854\t            return 0\n855\t\n856\t        line = {**common2, \"status\": status2, \"question\": question2}\n857\t        print(json.dumps(line))\n858\t        return 0\n859\t\n860\t    common = report_fields(model, events)\n861\t    line = {**common, \"status\": status, \"question\": question}\n862\t    print(json.dumps(line))\n863\t    return 0\n864\t\n865\t\n866\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n867\t    sys.exit(main())\n868\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ae622d81-efb9-4dda-92a0-5ebf75b0d73f","uuid":"efdd0ea8-caed-478e-90e4-5cd8a8c17c6a","timestamp":"2026-09-26T03:41:52.303Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/davidbean/Projects/attavo-worktree-219/adapters/codex/run","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"adapters/codex/run -- see README.md (this directory) for the two\nconfiguration keys, and ../README.md for the shared adapter contract.\n\nThe capability read (issue #41, recorded on the issue 2026-09-08 against\ncodex-cli 0.153.4): `codex exec review --output-schema <FILE>` ignores\nthe schema entirely -- confirmed live against a real planted-defect\ndiff, its `-o` output is always free-text prose, never JSON, whatever\nschema is named. This adapter therefore never invokes `codex exec\nreview`; both stages feed their own instruction to plain `codex exec -`\non stdin (confirmed live: this path does honor --output-schema, and\n`-p` is codex's `--profile`, not a prompt flag, so stdin is the only\nchannel and it is a positional `-`, not a flag).\n\nTwo channels, not one. `--json` prints events to stdout as JSONL\n(thread.started, turn.started, item.*, and a terminal turn.completed or\nturn.failed carrying usage) -- read for the model's own account of\nusage and for the terminal event; codex's own exit code is 1 on\nturn.failed, 0 on turn.completed, read directly, so this adapter reads\nthe terminal event for the reason, not for the verdict. The structured\nanswer comes from `--output-schema <FILE>` plus `--output-last-message\n<FILE>`, written as one JSON object once the turn completes -- that is\nwhere findings and tests_named come from; parsing them out of the event\nstream would be the wrong shape.\n\nNo model id appears anywhere codex reports -- not the event stream, not\n`codex doctor`, not any config file (confirmed live 2026-09-08; there is\nno ~/.codex/config.toml on this machine at all). [executor.codex].model\nis therefore required, not optional: this adapter refuses before ever\ninvoking codex if it is absent, and reports back exactly the id it was\ngiven, never one derived from output.\n\nauth mirrors claude-code's auth = \"subscription\": the operator's own\nmachine is logged in via a ChatGPT plan, and an OPENAI_API_KEY left in\nthe environment would silently switch codex to API billing on a run\nmeant for the plan. [executor.codex].auth names the mode this\ninvocation is trusted to run under, checked against OPENAI_API_KEY\nalone (deterministic and testable without a live `codex doctor` call,\nwhich a canned-response or faked-tool_binary test situation cannot\nanswer honestly anyway) -- this adapter refuses rather than silently\nfalling back when they disagree, because a wrong credential here is a\nbill, not just a wrong answer. cost_usd is therefore always null on\nthis adapter (no dollar figure appears anywhere under plan auth, and\nnone is invented for API auth either): a codex record carrying a\nnon-null cost would be evidence the wrong credential was used, a\nfinding about the credential, not a measurement.\n\nNot exercised live by the test suite (judgment is measured, not\ntested); its argument construction is checked by ATTAVO_ADAPTER_DRY_RUN\n(prints {\"argv\", \"instruction\", \"schema\"} instead of running -- argv\nalone would carry no instruction, since it goes in on stdin), and its\nresponse handling is checked by ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE, which\nfeeds a canned {\"events\": [...JSONL objects...], \"final\": {...the\noutput-file's parsed content, or null...}, \"exit_code\": N} instead of\ninvoking codex -- codex's own canned-response hook, since codex itself\nhas none (issue #41). A JSON array of such objects is a queue, one per\ninvocation this adapter makes, its last element repeating once\nexhausted; a single object still repeats for every call (issue #88: the\nnon-question retry below is a second invocation inside the same run).\n\nIssue #88 (class A: a report is not a question). Exactly claude-code's\nown fix, against the same build-side status/question channel: a\n\"question\" whose text clears MIN_QUESTION_CHARS but carries no \"?\" is a\nreport, not a question, and this adapter re-invokes codex once more\n(REINSTRUCTION appended to the same instruction) before ever reporting a\nhalt. A second report in a row still halts -- someone must look -- but\nits report line carries \"class\": \"non-question\" and both raw results\nalready reached stdout above it.\n\nIssue #88 (class B: work that outlives the invocation). `codex exec`'s\nown surface -- `codex exec --help` (codex-cli 0.153.4), the JSONL event\nstream (thread.*, turn.*, item.*), and the --output-schema/\n--output-last-message response -- names no subagent or background-task\nconcept at all: codex is a single agent driving sandboxed shell\ncommands, with no tool of its own that could spawn work outliving this\ninvocation the way claude-code's Agent/Workflow tools did (issue #88,\nconfirmed live on claude-code, this adapter's own conformance.json\ndeclares codex's guard unsupported for exactly this reason rather than\nasserting a check against a concept that is not there).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tempfile\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# the helper beside adapters/, imported without leaving a __pycache__ there\nsys.dont_write_bytecode = True\nsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent))\nfrom executor_env import executor_env  # noqa: E402\n\nSTATUS_SCHEMA = {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n    \"required\": [\"status\", \"question\"],\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"status\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"done\", \"question\"]},\n        \"question\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n    },\n}\n\nVERIFY_SCHEMA = {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n    \"required\": [\"findings\", \"tests_named\"],\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"findings\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"additionalProperties\": False,\n                \"required\": [\"title\", \"specimen\", \"retires\", \"rejection_test\", \"severity\"],\n                \"properties\": {\n                    \"title\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                    \"specimen\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                    \"retires\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                    \"rejection_test\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                    \"severity\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                },\n            },\n        },\n        \"tests_named\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1}},\n    },\n}\n\n# The counsel objective (issue #100): a stop is read inside the product\n# first, before a person. Identical to claude-code's own COUNSEL_SCHEMA --\n# the counsel structured answer is the shared adapter contract, not\n# either tool's own shape.\nCOUNSEL_SCHEMA = {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n    \"required\": [\"class\", \"verdict\", \"citations\", \"proposal\"],\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"class\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n        \"verdict\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"answer\", \"patch\", \"person\"]},\n        \"citations\": {\n            \"type\": \"array\",\n            \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"additionalProperties\": False,\n                \"required\": [\"kind\", \"path\", \"symbol\", \"ruling_id\"],\n                \"properties\": {\n                    \"kind\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"instruction\", \"mechanism\", \"guard\"]},\n                    \"path\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n                    \"symbol\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n                    \"ruling_id\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n                },\n            },\n        },\n        \"proposal\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"additionalProperties\": False,\n            \"required\": [\"ruling_text\", \"patch\", \"question\", \"issue\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n                \"ruling_text\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n                \"patch\": {\n                    \"type\": [\"object\", \"null\"],\n                    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n                    \"required\": [\"file\", \"symbol\", \"rejection_test\", \"diff\"],\n                    \"properties\": {\n                        \"file\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                        \"symbol\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                        \"rejection_test\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                        \"diff\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                    },\n                },\n                \"question\": {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n                # issue #175: the fourth proposal shape, for a \"patch\" or\n                # \"person\" verdict whose remedy is better filed as an item\n                # than shipped as a diff or asked as a bare question.\n                \"issue\": {\n                    \"type\": [\"object\", \"null\"],\n                    \"additionalProperties\": False,\n                    \"required\": [\"title\", \"body\"],\n                    \"properties\": {\n                        \"title\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                        \"body\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"minLength\": 1},\n                    },\n                },\n            },\n        },\n    },\n}\n\n\ndef read_text(path_env: str) -> str:\n    path = os.environ.get(path_env)\n    if not path:\n        return \"\"\n    p = Path(path)\n    return p.read_text() if p.is_file() else \"\"\n\n\ndef describe_file(path: str, text: str, diff_like: bool) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Path, byte count, line count, and (for a diff) files touched --\n    named in the instruction instead of inlining the file's own text, so\n    the instruction stays a small fixed size whatever the file's size\n    (issue #86: a 27,367-line deletion diff inlined whole exceeded\n    codex's own input ceiling). Files touched counts unified-diff file\n    headers (`diff --git a/... b/...`), the shape `git diff` produces.\"\"\"\n    size = Path(path).stat().st_size\n    lines = text.count(\"\\n\") + (1 if text and not text.endswith(\"\\n\") else 0)\n    if not diff_like:\n        return f\"`{path}` ({size} bytes, {lines} lines)\"\n    files = sum(1 for l in text.splitlines() if l.startswith(\"diff --git \"))\n    return f\"`{path}` ({size} bytes, {lines} lines, {files} files touched)\"\n\n\ndef spec_sections() -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"The spec's Problem, Claims, and Criteria sections verbatim -- the\n    same contract adapters/claude-code/run reads, since what a verifier\n    must be told is a product fact, not a tool-specific one.\"\"\"\n    spec_dir = Path(os.environ[\"ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR\"])\n    lines = (spec_dir / \"spec.md\").read_text().splitlines()\n    idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith(\"## \")]\n    headings = {lines[i].strip(): i for i in idx}\n    bounds = sorted(idx) + [len(lines)]\n\n    def section(heading: str) -> str:\n        start = headings[heading]\n        end = bounds[bounds.index(start) + 1] if start in bounds else len(lines)\n        return \"\\n\".join(lines[start:end]).strip()\n\n    return [section(\"## Problem\"), section(\"## Claims\"), section(\"## Criteria\")]\n\n\ndef stub_note() -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"None when ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR carries no stub/; else the sentence\n    naming what a stub is (issue #90, closed by #93). The stub is\n    evidence -- a criterion it satisfies trivially is weak -- but twice\n    the judge reported\n    a built tree as failing to match its stub as if the stub were the\n    intended change (tenant zero #664, #644): one sentence of vocabulary\n    was missing, not a smarter judge or a dropped stub.\"\"\"\n    stub_dir = Path(os.environ[\"ATTAVO_SPEC_DIR\"]) / \"stub\"\n    if not stub_dir.is_dir():\n        return None\n    return (\n        f\"## The stub at `{stub_dir}`\\n\\n\"\n        \"The stub is the fixture the acceptance criteria were shown to \"\n        \"pass against; it is not the intended change. A difference \"\n        \"between the built tree and the stub is not a finding: a \"\n        \"criterion the stub satisfies trivially is.\")\n\n\ndef build_instruction() -> str:\n    parts = list(spec_sections())\n\n    rulings = read_text(\"ATTAVO_RULINGS\")\n    if rulings.strip():\n        parts.append(\"## Rulings\\n\\n\" + rulings.strip())\n\n    feedback_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_FEEDBACK\")\n    feedback = read_text(\"ATTAVO_FEEDBACK\")\n    if feedback.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## Feedback from the previous attempt\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The feedback is at {describe_file(feedback_path, feedback, diff_like=False)}. \"\n            \"It is not inlined here: read it yourself with your own tools \"\n            \"before continuing.\")\n\n    parts.append(\n        \"commit your work on the current branch; if you cannot proceed \"\n        \"without a decision that changes the criteria, do not guess: \"\n        \"answer with status question\")\n\n    test_command = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_TEST_COMMAND\")\n    if test_command:\n        bound = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_TEST_TIMEOUT_S\")\n        parts.append(\n            f\"to run tests while you work, run `{test_command}`: it runs the tests \"\n            \"selected for the files you have changed\"\n            + (f\" and stops at {bound} s\" if bound else \"\")\n            + \". Do not run the whole test suite or any whole-tree check yourself: \"\n            \"after you commit, the gates are run for you and any failure is \"\n            \"returned to you on the next iteration\")\n\n    return \"\\n\\n\".join(parts)\n\n\ndef verify_instruction() -> str:\n    parts = list(spec_sections())\n\n    note = stub_note()\n    if note:\n        parts.append(note)\n\n    diff_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_DIFF\")\n    diff = read_text(\"ATTAVO_DIFF\")\n    if diff.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## Diff since the approved spec\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The diff is at {describe_file(diff_path, diff, diff_like=True)}. \"\n            \"It is not inlined here: read it yourself with your own tools \"\n            \"before judging it.\")\n    else:\n        parts.append(\"## Diff since the approved spec\\n\\n(the diff is empty)\")\n\n    build_record_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD\")\n    build_record = read_text(\"ATTAVO_BUILD_RECORD\")\n    if build_record.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## The build record\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The build record is at {describe_file(build_record_path, build_record, diff_like=False)}. \"\n            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n\n    parts.append(\n        \"you are verifying a completed build, not building: make no edits, \"\n        \"write no files, commit nothing. Read the diff named above against \"\n        \"the criteria and name what the criteria do not assert but the diff \"\n        \"should still be judged on, and name the tests that would assert \"\n        \"it if they existed. A spec whose approval commit does not \"\n        \"describe the working tree the build produced is itself a \"\n        \"finding: the diff is judged against what was approved, and a \"\n        \"divergence between them is reportable rather than assumed \"\n        \"away. Report each as a finding: title, specimen (the file or \"\n        \"commit the finding is about), retires (what having this \"\n        \"finding on record replaces or closes -- every finding must \"\n        \"carry one, or it is refused unfiled), rejection_test (the test \"\n        \"that would catch a regression of it), and severity. Severity \"\n        \"is derived rather than judged: the version, counter, \"\n        \"condition, or state change that flips a finding from harmless \"\n        \"to blocking; an adjective is not a severity. An empty \"\n        \"findings list means you looked and found nothing to report, not \"\n        \"that you did not look.\")\n    return \"\\n\\n\".join(parts)\n\n\ndef counsel_instruction() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Issue #100 (extended by issue #175): a stop is read inside the\n    product first, before a person. Identical to claude-code's own\n    counsel_instruction -- the record, its log, the diff, the forge's\n    checks, the item's graph, the tenant manifest, and this product's own\n    root are all handed by path, named rather than inlined, the same\n    discipline verify_instruction already applies to the diff (issue\n    #86).\"\"\"\n    parts = list(spec_sections())\n\n    rulings = read_text(\"ATTAVO_RULINGS\")\n    if rulings.strip():\n        parts.append(\"## Rulings\\n\\n\" + rulings.strip())\n\n    note = stub_note()\n    if note:\n        parts.append(note)\n\n    record_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_RECORD\")\n    record_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_RECORD\")\n    if record_text.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## The stage record\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The record is at {describe_file(record_path, record_text, diff_like=False)}. \"\n            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n\n    log_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_LOG\")\n    log_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_LOG\")\n    if log_text.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## The record's log\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The log is at {describe_file(log_path, log_text, diff_like=False)}. \"\n            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n\n    diff_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_DIFF\")\n    diff_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_DIFF\")\n    if diff_text.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## The item's diff since approval\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The diff is at {describe_file(diff_path, diff_text, diff_like=True)}. \"\n            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n\n    # issue #175: the forge in the evidence set. ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS is\n    # always written, a JSON {read, reason, checks} envelope -- read false\n    # names the missing manifest key rather than leaving the section out,\n    # since an unread forge must never be mistaken for a green one.\n    checks_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS\")\n    checks_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_CHECKS\")\n    if checks_text.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## The forge's checks on this stop's sha\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The checks are at {describe_file(checks_path, checks_text, diff_like=False)}: \"\n            \"a JSON object -- read: false means the forge was not read at \"\n            \"all (reason names the missing manifest key; treat this as \"\n            \"unknown, never as green) -- read: true carries one entry per \"\n            \"check, each naming, for a check that did not succeed, the \"\n            \"path of its own failed-step log. Read the failing check's \"\n            \"log before answering about the forge; a remedy that depends \"\n            \"on the forge's state cites the check and its log line.\")\n\n    # issue #175: the graph in the evidence set -- the default branch's\n    # own log since this item's branch point, this branch's own commits,\n    # the markers and records present at the stop's sha, and the check\n    # runs on the last three shas, so \"the checks are on the record\n    # commit, not the code commit\" is readable without a terminal.\n    graph_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_GRAPH\")\n    graph_text = read_text(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_GRAPH\")\n    if graph_text.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## The item's graph\\n\\n\"\n            f\"The graph is at {describe_file(graph_path, graph_text, diff_like=False)}. \"\n            \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n\n    manifest_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_MANIFEST\", \"\")\n    parts.append(f\"## The tenant manifest\\n\\nThe manifest is at `{manifest_path}`. \"\n                 \"Read it yourself with your own tools.\")\n\n    product_root = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_PRODUCT_ROOT\", \"\")\n    parts.append(\n        \"## The product's own root\\n\\n\"\n        f\"The product that ran this stage is at `{product_root}` \"\n        \"(`bin/attavo`, `adapters/`, `schema/`, `templates/`). A stop \"\n        \"whose cause is the product's own mechanism, not the tenant's, \"\n        \"is read here.\")\n\n    # issue #175: attavo counsel N --ask hands the operator's own text as\n    # the question to answer, over the same evidence set, rather than\n    # diagnosing a stop -- the record above is context, not necessarily a\n    # failure.\n    question = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_COUNSEL_QUESTION\", \"\")\n    if question.strip():\n        parts.append(\n            \"## The operator's question\\n\\n\" + question.strip() +\n            \"\\n\\nAnswer this question using the evidence above; the \"\n            \"record named above is context, not necessarily a failure.\")\n\n    parts.append(\n        \"you are counseling a stop, read-only: make no edits, write no \"\n        \"files, commit nothing. Read the record, its log, the spec, the \"\n        \"rulings, the diff, the forge's checks, the item's graph, the \"\n        \"tenant manifest, and the product's own root named above with \"\n        \"your own tools, then answer with exactly one class (the stop's \"\n        \"class, or \\\"unclassed\\\"), one verdict -- \\\"answer\\\" (the stop is \"\n        \"answerable inside the rules: a ruling text, in \"\n        \"proposal.ruling_text, with three citations -- instruction, \"\n        \"mechanism, guard, each a path and symbol or a ruling id), \"\n        \"\\\"patch\\\" (the stop is a product defect: the mechanism's file \"\n        \"and symbol, a rejection test, and a unified diff, in \"\n        \"proposal.patch -- or, when the remedy is better filed as an \"\n        \"item than shipped as a diff, a title and a body in this \"\n        \"repository's own issue format -- a Severity/Provenance line, \"\n        \"then Problem, Retires, Change, and Acceptance sections -- in \"\n        \"proposal.issue), or \\\"person\\\" (the answer would touch the \"\n        \"definition of done and no rule covers it: the question restated \"\n        \"in one line, in proposal.question -- or, when the remedy is \"\n        \"better filed as an item than asked as a bare question, a title \"\n        \"and a body in the same issue format, in proposal.issue) -- and \"\n        \"citations for whichever verdict you gave. You propose; you do \"\n        \"not act.\")\n    return \"\\n\\n\".join(parts)\n\n\ndef load_config() -> dict:\n    raw = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_CONFIG\")\n    return json.loads(raw) if raw else {}\n\n\n# codex's own ceiling (issue #93): \"Input exceeds the maximum length of\n# 1048576 characters\" -- confirmed live 2026-09-08 against codex-cli\n# 0.153.4, the same specimen issue #86 fixed by handing the diff over by\n# path (tenant zero #659, verify-01: input_too_large, max_chars 1048576,\n# actual_chars 1297762). Declared here and in conformance.json's own\n# input_limit so a test can assert the two agree.\nINPUT_LIMIT_CHARS = 1_048_576\n\n\ndef input_limit_chars() -> int:\n    \"\"\"The declared ceiling, chars. Overridable only for tests via\n    ATTAVO_ADAPTER_INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS -- never a tenant knob: the ceiling\n    is a fact about codex, not something [executor.codex] can set (a\n    tenant typing a number it does not know is the pattern rule 42\n    refuses).\"\"\"\n    override = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS\")\n    return int(override) if override else INPUT_LIMIT_CHARS\n\n\ndef ceiling_refusal(instruction: str) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"None if instruction stays at or under codex's declared ceiling;\n    else the refusal naming both numbers, checked before codex is ever\n    invoked -- so an oversized prompt never reaches the tool that would\n    refuse it anyway, three seconds and a wasted call later.\"\"\"\n    limit = input_limit_chars()\n    size = len(instruction)\n    if size <= limit:\n        return None\n    return (f\"ceiling exceeded -- instruction is {size} chars, over \"\n           f\"codex's declared limit of {limit} chars\")\n\n\ndef build_argv(schema_path: Path, output_path: Path, config: dict, stage: str) -> list[str]:\n    # verify and counsel (issue #100) are mechanically read-only, not\n    # just instructed to be: the sandbox enforces \"make no edits, write\n    # no files, commit nothing\" rather than trusting the instruction\n    # alone. Any stage other than \"build\" gets the read-only sandbox.\n    sandbox = \"workspace-write\" if stage == \"build\" else \"read-only\"\n    return [\"codex\", \"exec\", \"-\",\n           \"--model\", config[\"model\"],\n           \"--sandbox\", sandbox,\n           \"--output-schema\", str(schema_path),\n           \"--output-last-message\", str(output_path),\n           \"--json\",\n           \"--ignore-user-config\", \"--ignore-rules\"]\n\n\ndef check_auth(config: dict) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"None if the declared auth mode and OPENAI_API_KEY's presence\n    agree; else the mismatch, refused before codex is ever invoked.\"\"\"\n    auth = config.get(\"auth\", \"subscription\")\n    has_key = bool(os.environ.get(\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"))\n    if auth == \"subscription\" and has_key:\n        return (\"auth is \\\"subscription\\\" but OPENAI_API_KEY is set in the \"\n                \"environment -- codex would silently bill the API account \"\n                \"instead of running under the plan\")\n    if auth == \"api\" and not has_key:\n        return \"auth is \\\"api\\\" but OPENAI_API_KEY is not set in the environment\"\n    return None\n\n\ndef parse_events(stdout: str) -> list[dict]:\n    \"\"\"One JSON object per JSONL line; a tracing line (e.g. \"ERROR\n    codex_api::...\") is not JSON and is skipped, not an error -- codex\n    writes both to the same stream under --json.\"\"\"\n    events = []\n    for line in stdout.splitlines():\n        line = line.strip()\n        if not line:\n            continue\n        try:\n            events.append(json.loads(line))\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n    return events\n\n\ndef terminal_event(events: list[dict]) -> dict | None:\n    for e in events:\n        if e.get(\"type\") in (\"turn.completed\", \"turn.failed\"):\n            return e\n    return None\n\n\nEVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS = 400\n\n\ndef append_event(events_path: str, kind: str, text: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"One {at, kind, text} JSON line appended to events_path and\n    flushed at once -- the same shape and the same open-write-close\n    discipline bin/attavo's own append_event and claude-code's own copy\n    use, kept independently here since an adapter is a separate process\n    with no import path into the product or into another adapter.\"\"\"\n    line = {\"at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ\"),\n           \"kind\": kind, \"text\": \" \".join(text.split())[:EVENT_TEXT_MAX_CHARS]}\n    with open(events_path, \"a\") as fh:\n        fh.write(json.dumps(line) + \"\\n\")\n\n\ndef first_line(text: str) -> str:\n    stripped = text.strip()\n    return stripped.splitlines()[0] if stripped else \"\"\n\n\ndef item_events(obj: dict) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:\n    \"\"\"[(kind, text), ...] for one parsed codex --json stream line --\n    zero, one, or two (a command_execution item carries both the\n    command and, once it has run, its own output, so it maps to a\n    tool_call and a tool_result together). An agent_message item's own\n    text maps to \"message\" -- confirmed live (issue #41, recorded\n    2026-09-08 against codex-cli 0.153.4): conformance.json's own\n    \"done\" situation, `{\"type\": \"item.completed\", \"item\": {\"type\":\n    \"agent_message\", \"text\": \"ok\"}}`, is this exact shape. A\n    command_execution item's \"command\" maps to \"tool_call\" (the\n    command's own first line) and its \"aggregated_output\", once\n    present, to \"tool_result\" (the output's own first line) -- both\n    field names are confirmed, by tenant zero's own verify logs on\n    attavo/code-factory since #649: every command_execution item there\n    carries exactly the keys aggregated_output, command, exit_code, id,\n    status, type, the same shape tests/test_adapter_codex.py's recorded\n    fixture exercises this mapping through. Every other item type\n    (reasoning, file_change, mcp_tool_call, and the rest), and every\n    non-item.completed line (thread.*, turn.*, \"result\" itself), maps\n    to nothing here.\"\"\"\n    if obj.get(\"type\") != \"item.completed\":\n        return []\n    item = obj.get(\"item\") or {}\n    kind = item.get(\"type\")\n    events: list[tuple[str, str]] = []\n    if kind == \"agent_message\" and item.get(\"text\"):\n        events.append((\"message\", first_line(item[\"text\"])))\n    elif kind == \"command_execution\":\n        command = item.get(\"command\")\n        if command:\n            events.append((\"tool_call\", first_line(str(command))))\n        output = item.get(\"aggregated_output\")\n        if output:\n            events.append((\"tool_result\", first_line(str(output))))\n    return events\n\n\ndef write_events(events_path: str, parsed_events: list[dict]) -> None:\n    for obj in parsed_events:\n        for kind, text in item_events(obj):\n            append_event(events_path, kind, text)\n\n\ndef load_canned_responses() -> list[dict] | None:\n    \"\"\"None when ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE is unset. Otherwise the\n    queue of canned {\"events\", \"final\", \"exit_code\"} objects this run\n    answers from: a single object repeats for every invocation this\n    adapter makes (unchanged since v0.3.0 -- most tests make exactly\n    one), a JSON array is consumed in order, one response per\n    invocation, its last element repeating once exhausted (issue #88:\n    the non-question retry is a second invocation inside the same\n    adapter run, and a canned test needs to hand it a different answer\n    than the first).\"\"\"\n    response_file = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE\")\n    if not response_file:\n        return None\n    raw = json.loads(Path(response_file).read_text())\n    return raw if isinstance(raw, list) else [raw]\n\n\ndef invoke(instruction: str, schema: dict, config: dict, stage: str,\n          responses: list[dict] | None, call_index: int\n          ) -> tuple[str, list[dict], dict | None, int]:\n    \"\"\"(raw transcript text, parsed events, the output file's parsed\n    content or None, exit code) -- from responses[call_index] (clamped\n    to the last element) when responses is not None, else a real\n    `codex exec -` invocation.\"\"\"\n    if responses is not None:\n        canned = responses[min(call_index, len(responses) - 1)]\n        events = canned.get(\"events\") or []\n        final = canned.get(\"final\")\n        exit_code = canned.get(\"exit_code\", 0)\n        raw = \"\\n\".join(json.dumps(e) for e in events)\n        return raw, events, final, exit_code\n\n    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:\n        schema_path = Path(tmp) / \"schema.json\"\n        schema_path.write_text(json.dumps(schema))\n        output_path = Path(tmp) / \"output.json\"\n\n        argv = build_argv(schema_path, output_path, config, stage)\n        run = subprocess.run(argv, input=instruction, capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=False, env=executor_env())\n        raw = run.stdout\n        if run.returncode != 0 and run.stderr.strip():\n            raw = (raw + \"\\n\" if raw else \"\") + run.stderr.rstrip(\"\\n\")\n\n        final = None\n        if output_path.is_file():\n            try:\n                final = json.loads(output_path.read_text())\n            except ValueError:\n                final = None\n        return raw, parse_events(run.stdout), final, run.returncode\n\n\ndef adapter_version() -> str:\n    try:\n        out = subprocess.run([\"codex\", \"--version\"], capture_output=True,\n                             text=True, check=False)\n    except OSError:\n        return \"\"\n    return out.stdout.strip()\n\n\nMIN_QUESTION_CHARS = 40\n\nREINSTRUCTION = (\n    \"your last answer was a report, not a question; either continue to \"\n    \"done, or ask one question ending in a question mark that names the \"\n    \"spec line or guard you cannot resolve\")\n\n\ndef question_class(status: str, question: str | None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\"done\" (no halt at all), \"fail\" (a question too short or absent to\n    be worth a person's judgment -- MIN_QUESTION_CHARS, unchanged since\n    v0.15.0), \"report\" (long enough but carries no \"?\" -- issue #88's\n    class A, a status update in the question channel), or \"question\" (a\n    real question). Identical to claude-code's own -- the build-side\n    status/question channel is the shared adapter contract, not either\n    tool's own shape.\"\"\"\n    if status != \"question\":\n        return \"done\"\n    if not question or len(question) < MIN_QUESTION_CHARS:\n        # An absent or too-short question is a report with nothing in\n        # it: a status update in the question channel (issue #150, five\n        # specimens on 2026-09-17), never an exit with no report line.\n        return \"report\"\n    if \"?\" not in question:\n        return \"report\"\n    return \"question\"\n\n\ndef usage_fields(events: list[dict]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"(tokens.input, tokens.output) for one call's own event stream --\n    split out from report_fields so a retry's first, discarded call\n    still counts (issue #88: a non-question costs two executor turns,\n    and a report line built from the second call alone undercounts by\n    exactly the first one).\"\"\"\n    term = terminal_event(events)\n    usage = (term or {}).get(\"usage\") or {}\n    return usage.get(\"input_tokens\", 0) or 0, usage.get(\"output_tokens\", 0) or 0\n\n\ndef report_fields(model: str, events: list[dict]) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"The fields common to every final report line, derived from one\n    call's own event stream -- model is always the configured id (issue\n    #41: codex reports none of its own), cost_usd always null (issue\n    #41's auth addendum).\"\"\"\n    tokens_input, tokens_output = usage_fields(events)\n    return {\n        \"model\": model,\n        \"model_version\": None,\n        \"adapter_version\": adapter_version(),\n        \"tokens\": {\"input\": tokens_input, \"output\": tokens_output},\n        \"cost_usd\": None,\n    }\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    config = load_config()\n    stage = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_STAGE\", \"build\")\n    verifying = stage == \"verify\"\n    counseling = stage == \"counsel\"\n    if verifying:\n        instruction, schema = verify_instruction(), VERIFY_SCHEMA\n    elif counseling:\n        instruction, schema = counsel_instruction(), COUNSEL_SCHEMA\n    else:\n        instruction, schema = build_instruction(), STATUS_SCHEMA\n\n    model = config.get(\"model\")\n    if not model:\n        sys.stderr.write(\n            \"adapters/codex/run: refused: [executor.codex].model is \"\n            \"required -- no model id appears anywhere codex itself \"\n            \"reports (the event stream, codex doctor, or any config file)\\n\")\n        return 1\n\n    ceiling = ceiling_refusal(instruction)\n    if ceiling:\n        sys.stderr.write(f\"adapters/codex/run: refused: {ceiling}\\n\")\n        return 1\n\n    if os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_DRY_RUN\"):\n        argv = build_argv(Path(\"<schema.json>\"), Path(\"<output.json>\"), config, stage)\n        print(json.dumps({\"argv\": argv, \"instruction\": instruction, \"schema\": schema}))\n        return 0\n\n    if not os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_ADAPTER_RESPONSE_FILE\"):\n        auth_error = check_auth(config)\n        if auth_error:\n            sys.stderr.write(f\"adapters/codex/run: refused: {auth_error}\\n\")\n            return 1\n\n    responses = load_canned_responses()\n    events_path = os.environ.get(\"ATTAVO_EVENTS\")\n\n    raw, events, final, exit_code = invoke(instruction, schema, config, stage, responses, 0)\n    # keep the evidence: both channels, not just the fields below, are\n    # the transcript build-NN.log saves -- a halt otherwise cannot be\n    # explained after the fact. final is printed here too (issue #88):\n    # a question the executor answers on the first call otherwise never\n    # reaches the transcript at all, since only the terminal report line\n    # -- printed once, at the very end -- carried it before.\n    print(raw.rstrip(\"\\n\") if raw.strip() else \"(codex produced no output)\")\n    if final is not None:\n        print(json.dumps(final))\n    if events_path:\n        write_events(events_path, events)\n\n    if exit_code != 0:\n        return exit_code\n\n    # An absent or unparseable output file is a failure whether this is\n    # a build or a verify -- the same class as claude-code's absent\n    # structured_output.\n    if final is None:\n        return 1\n\n    if verifying:\n        common = report_fields(model, events)\n        line = {**common,\n               \"findings\": final.get(\"findings\"),\n               \"tests_named\": final.get(\"tests_named\")}\n        print(json.dumps(line))\n        return 0\n\n    if counseling:\n        common = report_fields(model, events)\n        line = {**common,\n               \"class\": final.get(\"class\"),\n               \"verdict\": final.get(\"verdict\"),\n               \"citations\": final.get(\"citations\"),\n               \"proposal\": final.get(\"proposal\")}\n        print(json.dumps(line))\n        return 0\n\n    status = final.get(\"status\", \"done\")\n    question = final.get(\"question\")\n    cls = question_class(status, question)\n\n    if cls == \"fail\":\n        return 1\n\n    if cls == \"report\":\n        # Issue #88, class A: a report in the question channel is\n        # re-instructed once, in this same invocation, before it ever\n        # reaches a person.\n        reinstruction = instruction + \"\\n\\n\" + REINSTRUCTION\n        raw2, events2, final2, exit_code2 = invoke(\n            reinstruction, schema, config, stage, responses, 1)\n        print(raw2.rstrip(\"\\n\") if raw2.strip() else \"(codex produced no output)\")\n        if final2 is not None:\n            print(json.dumps(final2))\n        if events_path:\n            write_events(events_path, events2)\n\n        if exit_code2 != 0:\n            return exit_code2\n        if final2 is None:\n            return 1\n\n        status2 = final2.get(\"status\", \"done\")\n        question2 = final2.get(\"question\")\n        cls2 = question_class(status2, question2)\n        if cls2 == \"fail\":\n            return 1\n\n        common2 = report_fields(model, events2)\n        # A non-question costs two executor turns, not one: sum both\n        # calls' tokens into the one report line this invocation gets,\n        # whatever the retry's own outcome. cost_usd stays null (issue\n        # #41): null plus null is still null.\n        tokens1_input, tokens1_output = usage_fields(events)\n        common2[\"tokens\"][\"input\"] += tokens1_input\n        common2[\"tokens\"][\"output\"] += tokens1_output\n\n        if cls2 == \"report\":\n            # Two reports in a row: still a halt (someone must look),\n            # but the transcript above already carries both raw\n            # results, and this report line names the class so a\n            # reader -- or a future record field, not this one -- can\n            # tell it apart from a genuine question.\n            print(\"adapters/codex/run: halted: class non-question -- the \"\n                  \"executor reported instead of asking, twice in a row\")\n            line = {**common2, \"status\": \"question\", \"question\": question2,\n                   \"class\": \"non-question\"}\n            print(json.dumps(line))\n            return 0\n\n        line = {**common2, \"status\": status2, \"question\": question2}\n        print(json.dumps(line))\n        return 0\n\n    common = report_fields(model, events)\n    line = {**common, \"status\": status, \"question\": question}\n    print(json.dumps(line))\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n","numLines":868,"startLine":1,"totalLines":868}}}
